WEBVTT
Kind: descriptions
Language: en
NOTE Narration script, timed to phase2_demo.mp4; shorter than the on-screen captions so it fits each cue.

1
00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:09.400
CS702 phase two prototype: Interactive Debugging and Steering of Multi-Agent AI Systems, re-implementing AGDebugger. Team: Nguyen Nhat Minh and Do Duc Anh.

2
00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:20.400
The problem: teams of LLM agents exchange dozens of messages. One wrong step cascades, and a long run ends with a wrong answer or none. Logs do not show where it went wrong, or let you try a fix.

3
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:31.400
Users are developers tuning multi-agent systems. Their goal: find the message that derailed a run and steer the team to the right result, by browsing the history, sending messages, resetting and editing.

4
00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:53.400
Our prototype re-implements the paper's three components: message history with sending and execution control; reset-and-edit with session forking on real checkpoints; and an overview visualisation. It is our own implementation on AutoGen 0.7.5 with a React front end. The team under debug is a Magentic-One orchestrator with a Coder, an Executor and a WebSearcher, running Claude Haiku 5.5 at low effort.

5
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:04.000
We load a recorded failing run, replayed from saved history and checkpoints with no model calls.

6
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:12.000
Component one: every runtime message is a card with sender, recipient or topic, message type and payload, including tool calls and results.

7
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:19.000
Long payloads expand in place; the coloured bar marks the sending agent.

8
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:28.000
After two hundred and twenty-eight messages the run ends without an answer. The expected result is 6651.7: financial-year visitors divided by six instruments.

9
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:37.600
Sending: compose a broadcast or a direct message; it lands in the runtime queue.

10
00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:42.000
Run processes the queue; Step processes one message; Drop discards the next.

11
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.200
Dropped. The agents never saw it.

12
00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:51.400
Component three: a column per session, a rectangle per message.

13
00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:54.400
Colour by sender.

14
00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:57.400
Colour by recipient.

15
00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:00.700
Colour by message type: tool calls stand out.

16
00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:04.400
Hover shows a message; a click scrolls the history to it.

17
00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:07.400
The legend filters by type.

18
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:15.000
Component two. The mistake is at message nine: the orchestrator's plan assumes a published financial-year report.

19
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.500
We rewrite the plan to point at the monthly reports.

20
00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:28.300
Reset-and-edit restores every agent from the checkpoint, re-publishes the edited plan and forks session one.

21
00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:31.000
Step runs one message live.

22
00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:41.800
The edited broadcast is processed; Run continues the branch.

23
00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:49.600
The steered branch finishes: the same team sums the monthly figures and answers 6651.7.

24
00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:54.200
The overview shows both sessions; the amber marker is the fork point.

25
00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:58.500
The original failing branch remains browsable for comparison.

26
00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:12.000
Status: runtime, checkpoints and components one and two fully work; the overview is basic. Opus never failed in ninety-seven runs; natural failures come from the Haiku team. Next: more failing runs, E1, and the study.
